                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{G} = U(1)
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} smooth ∞-groupoids
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi 𝔾
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}(\mathbf{B}\mathbb{G}_{\mathrm{conn}})
            classifies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundle+with+connection"} circle 2-bundle with connection
            without 2-form part: in parts of the literature this is known as “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+gerbes"} bundle gerbes
            with connective structure but without
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curving"} curving
          %p
            So the general definition considered here assigns a higher Atiyah groupoid to a “bundle gerbe with connective structure but no
            = succeed "\u201D." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curving"} curving
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+2-groupoid"} Courant 2-groupoid
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+integration"} Lie integrates
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+Courant+Lie+2-algebroid"} standard Courant Lie 2-algebroid
            traditionally induced by this data.
          %p
            The notion of higher Atiyah groupoids is more general still: the definition does not really require that the object fed into the construction is a plain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} principal ∞-bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+%E2%88%9E-connection"} principal ∞-connection
            (hence
            %em with
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curving"}> curving
            ”). We show below that the corresponding higher Atiyah groupoid is that groupoid object whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group+of+bisections"} ∞-group of bisections
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+n-group"} quantomorphism n-group
            of the principal
            = succeed "-connection" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+n-bundle"} prequantum n-bundle
          %p
            In summary, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometric
            generalization of the notion of Atiyah groupoids unifies all three of the traditional notions of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Atiyah+groupoid"} Atiyah groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Courant+2-groupoid"} Courant 2-groupoid
            and of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantomorphism+group"} quantomorphism group
            and refines each of these to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+geometry"} higher geometry
